Here's the basis of the comparison, as I made it. I'm going to shift
from discussing the character's point of view to discussing a
hypothetical unspoiled player's point of view, because a single game
is not likely to have enough shops/items for a single character to
reproduce the shopping spoilers. To intuit the details of a shopping
spoiler, an unspoiled player would need to:

1) Recognize* that some pricing structure is used (prices are not
merely assigned randomly for everything);
2) Recognize that all shopkeepers are using the same pricing
structure;
3) Recognize that pricing has a factor based on charisma, and figure
out what that factor is;
4) Recognize that pricing is sometimes adjusted purely at random*;
5) Recognize that the random factor is always the same percentage;
6) Deduce the actual (base) prices of everything based on knowledge of
the above factors.

By contrast, with regard to recognizing the usefulness of
curse-catchers, an unspoiled player would have to:
1) Experience the fact that high level monsters can curse objects in
your inventory, and they can get cursed as well at intervals on the
ascension run;
2) Recognize that various single items, or groups of items that are
all exactly the same, get cursed at random whenever this event happens
(while items within containers do not);
3) Realize that if you have items in your main inventory whose BUC
status doesn't matter, their presence would reduce the percentage
chance that items whose BUC status does matter would get cursed, and
act on that knowledge.

To me, the complexity of the first scheme makes it much harder, in the
end, to determine inductively. Several factors are being used, and can
act at cross purposes or multiply effects; all the player has to go on
is the price he's being quoted--he doesn't know what factors have gone
into quoting that price. In the second scheme, what seems random
actually is, so there is nothing more to determine.

I'm hardly an unspoiled player, but I tend to play without referring
to spoilers (beyond what I can recall). I gather, in a general way,
that certain items are cheap and others are expensive, and of course I
know how to find scrolls of ID (which are set apart from the pack),
but beyond that, prices feel rather arbitrary. And if I play a
character with high charisma, it plays havoc with my expectations of
what things are going to cost. (I assume, btw, that that's the effect
that the devteam intended.) Put more simply, it's easier to assume,
and act on the assumption, that things are arbitrary, than it is to
assume, and act on the assumption, that things are not, when there are
a number of factors that create the appearance of arbitrariness.


*Every item for which I've used the word "Recognize" would involve
forming a hypothesis based on experiential data and coming to use it
as a working assumption. Strictly speaking, one's assurance that this
hypothesis is correct could never reach 100%.

**I'm assuming here that for all practical purposes, pseudorandom ==
random, which I think it's safe to say it is.
